      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2832.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 673,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining characteristic of our planet and the fundamental building block of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, based on its properties, states, and importance:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Unique Physical Properties of Water\nWater is famous among scientists for being bizarre. Unlike most substances, water behaves in unusual ways that make life possible:\n\n*   **The Three States of Matter:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states: \n    *   **Liquid** (the oceans, rivers, rain)\n    *   **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n    *   **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of the way its electrical charge is distributed (it is a polar molecule), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can easily carry minerals, nutrients, and chemicals through our bodies and through the soil.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, **expands** when it turns to ice, making it less dense than liquid water. This is why ice cubes float. In nature, this is crucial: lakes freeze from the top down, creating an insulating layer of ice that allows aquatic life to survive the winter underneath.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human body temperatures stable.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers roughly **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas. \n*   **Saltwater:** About **96.5%** of Earth's water is in the oceans and is too salty for humans to drink or use for crops without expensive desalination processes.\n*   **Freshwater:** Only about **2.5%** is freshwater. Most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nBiology and water are inseparable:\n*   **The Human Body:** An adult human body is made up of roughly **55% to 60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are roughly 31% water.\n*   **Metabolism:** Water is the medium in which all the cellular and chemical reactions of life take place. Without water, nutrients cannot be transported through the bloodstream, waste cannot be flushed out, and cells cannot maintain their structure.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts our planet’s geography, drives our weather and climate, and flows through the veins of every living thing."
